      In the proses page, when we get the playlist from the API, we also store the full list of candidates (or at least the list of songs that were considered) in localStorage.\n\n    However, the requirement says not to change the EDAS flow. Storing extra data in localStorage from the proses page does not change the EDAS flow (which is server-side). It only changes what we store on the client.\n\n    Let's do:\n\n      In the proses page, after we get the playlist from `fetchEdasPlaylist`, we also store the full candidate list (if we had it) but we don't. The `fetchEdasPlaylist` function only returns the playlist.\n\n    We don't have the candidate list in the proses page either.\n\n    Therefore, we must change the `fetchEdasPlaylist` function to return more data? But that would be changing the EDAS flow? The endpoint `/api/dummy-playlist` is called by the proses page. If we change what it returns, we are changing the EDAS flow? Possibly.\n\n    Given the instructions, I think we are allowed to change the client-side code to store more data, but we are not allowed to change the server-side EDAS algorithm or the main flow (like the NLG, history, etc.). The endpoint `/api/dummy-playlist` is likely a mock endpoint that returns a fixed playlist for development. In production, it might be replaced by the real EDAS.\n\n    Since we are in a development environment, we can change the endpoint to return more data? But the requirement says not to change the EDAS flow. We must be cautious.\n\n    Alternatively, we can note that the current playlist in the hasil page is the top N songs from the EDAS ranking. We can assume that the next best songs (beyond the top N) are the candidates for replacement. We don't have them, but we can try to approximate by generating variations? That's complex.\n\n    Given the time, I propose a simpler approach that might meet the requirement in spirit:\n\n      We will allow the user to select songs to remove. We will then remove them and then